  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nRidging is building aloft over the region today but once again cloud \ncover may be stubborn in hanging around. Have therefore lowered \nhighs again today based on the performance of the last two days, \nkeeping highs in the lower to mid 70s. Ridging continues to build in \non Friday as we finally kick out most of the cloud cover, allowing \ntemperatures to climb back to the upper 70s to lower 80s. Dry \nconditions are expected until Sunday morning in SEMO when a \nshort wave squashes the ridge and brings back some precip \nchances (20-30% chance). \n\nRidging aloft rebuilds early next week pulling temperatures back \ninto the mid to upper 80s by Tuesday. The next system looks to be \nmid week next week, but there are some rather significant model \ndifferences currently. The EC has a northern stream shortwave trof \nover the upper Midwest/Canadian border and a southern stream closed \nlow over the Four Corners region by Tuesday night. There is also a \nlong sfc cold front draped from east-central Canada, through the \nupper Midwest, to the TX Panhandle. The GFS, on the other hand, has \na longwave trof through the Plains during this same time, with a sfc \nlow over northeast MO and an attendant cold front stretching down \nthrough east TX. Both of these solutions bring needed rain to the \narea but with very different timing and extent. For now NBM has PoPs \nmoving in from the west as early as Tuesday morning, and ramping up \nto widespread 50-60% by Wednesday afternoon and 40-50% area wide \nWednesday evening and overnight. But all of this would be low \nconfidence at this point due to the above model differences.",
